var tafsirData_31 = {
  "1": "“Alif-Lãm-Mĩm.” Once again a chapter opens with these isolated letters, recited as individual sounds. We have encountered this same combination at the start of the previous two chapters, and its reappearance here creates a thread of continuity across this section of the Quran. The disconnected letters serve as a threshold, pausing the reader before the verses that follow and inviting a posture of attentive openness.\n\nAs with the other occurrences, scholars have offered various interpretations over the centuries. Some considered these letters names of the chapters. Others saw in them a form of oath. A widely held view notes that the Quran is made of the very letters the Arabs used in their speech, yet they could not produce anything comparable, so the letters stand as a quiet challenge. Many scholars have taken the most cautious position: that their complete meaning is known to Allah, while affirming that they serve a clear purpose for the listener regardless.\n\nThe effect of these letters, as a chapter begins, is to arrest attention. They are unlike the opening of ordinary speech. They stop the hearer at the door of revelation and invite reflection before the message unfolds. Whatever else they signify, they announce that what follows is not casual human talk but a communication from the One who knows both the seen and the unseen.\n\nFor the believer, the fitting response is humble attentiveness rather than anxious speculation. The early Muslims recited these letters as part of the Quran, accepted them, and proceeded into the verses that followed. The reader today can do the same, receiving these letters as a reminder that the Quran is, in part, a book whose full depths exceed human grasp, and that the first act of faith is often simply to listen, to trust, and to enter the revelation with a heart ready to receive whatever the One who sent it wishes to teach.",
  "2": "“These are the verses of the Book, rich in wisdom.” Immediately after the isolated letters, this verse identifies the nature of what the reader is about to encounter. The Quran is described as “the Book,” a single, unified revelation, and its verses are said to be “rich in wisdom.” The word “wisdom” is central. The Quran is not merely a collection of commands or stories; it is a book full of wisdom, of deep, practical, life-shaping insight into how human beings are meant to live and what they are meant to know.\n\nThe phrase “rich in wisdom” suggests abundance. The wisdom of the Book is not sparse or hard to find; it is plentiful, woven throughout, available on every page. Every verse, whether it tells a story, lays down a law, describes the Hereafter, or offers a parable, carries wisdom within it. The richness means that the reader can return to the Book again and again and always find more, always discover fresh insight, always encounter guidance suited to the present moment.\n\nThis description also sets the tone for the chapter that follows. The chapter of Luqmân is particularly associated with wisdom, for it contains the teachings of Luqmân, a figure renowned for his wise counsel to his son. The opening description of the Book as “rich in wisdom” prepares the reader to receive that wisdom with reverence and attention, recognising that what follows is not casual reading but a treasury of insight meant to shape the mind, the heart, and the conduct.\n\nThe connection between the isolated letters of the previous verse and this description is itself meaningful. The letters, whose full meaning is known to God, are followed immediately by a statement of the Book’s wisdom. The mystery of the letters gives way to the clarity of the wisdom. Whatever remains hidden, the wisdom that is offered is plain, rich, and available to every reader who approaches with a sincere heart.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to approach the Quran as a treasury of wisdom rather than as a mere text to be read quickly and set aside. It calls us to read slowly, to reflect deeply, to draw out the insight embedded in each verse, and to let that wisdom shape how we think, how we live, and how we relate to God. The Book is rich; the question is whether we will take the time and bring the openness needed to gather its wealth, becoming people whose lives are increasingly shaped by the wisdom that God has so generously placed within the verses of His Book.",
  "3": "“It is a guide and mercy for the good-doers.” Having described the Book as rich in wisdom, this verse now names what that wisdom accomplishes for a particular group of people. The Book is “a guide and mercy,” but these gifts are specified as belonging to “the good-doers.” The Quran guides and shows mercy to all who approach it, yet its guidance takes root and its mercy is fully received by those whose hearts are already turned toward goodness, those who are willing not just to hear the truth but to act on it.\n\nThe pairing of “guide and mercy” is significant. Guidance without mercy could be cold and demanding; mercy without guidance could be vague and directionless. Together, they form a complete gift. The Book shows the way, and it does so with compassion, understanding the weakness of the human creature and carrying it gently toward the good. This combination reflects the character of God Himself, who is both the Guide who does not let us wander and the Merciful who does not crush us for our frailty.\n\nThe phrase “for the good-doers” identifies the ideal recipients. Good-doers are those whose faith expresses itself in action, who do not merely believe in the abstract but translate belief into conduct. For such people, the Book’s guidance finds fertile soil. They are ready to follow where it leads, and so its wisdom enters their lives and bears fruit. This does not mean the Book has nothing to offer others, but it highlights a spiritual principle: the degree to which the guidance benefits us depends on the degree to which we are willing to do the good it points toward.\n\nThis verse connects to the chapter’s theme of wisdom. Luqmân, whose counsel fills the heart of this chapter, is himself a model good-doer, a person of wisdom whose life expressed the guidance he received. The verse quietly prepares the reader to receive Luqmân’s wisdom as one of the good-doers, ready to be guided and shown mercy.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is both a promise and a condition. The promise is that the Quran is a guide and a mercy, a light for the path and a comfort for the heart. The condition is that this gift is fully received by the good-doers, those who respond with obedient action. The verse invites us to place ourselves among the good-doers, to approach the Book not as mere spectators but as willing followers, so that its guidance takes root in our lives and its mercy shapes our hearts, and we become, like Luqmân, people whose wisdom is proved by the goodness of our deeds.",
  "4": "“Those who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter.” This verse describes the good-doers of the previous verse in concrete terms, naming three defining practices. The first is the establishment of prayer. Prayer is the believer’s direct, regular communion with God, the rhythm by which each day is oriented around the remembrance of the Creator. To “establish” it means to make it a firm, faithful, central practice, not an occasional gesture. It is the heartbeat of the believer’s relationship with God.\n\nThe second practice is the paying of the alms-tax, known in Arabic as “zakah.” This is the systematic giving of a portion of one’s wealth to those in need, an act that purifies both the wealth and the giver. Zakah links worship with social responsibility, joining love of God with care for His creatures. The believer who establishes prayer, reaching up to God, also pays zakah, reaching out to people. The two belong together: a faith that looks up but does not reach out is incomplete.\n\nThe third quality is “sure faith in the Hereafter.” The good-doers do not merely suspect or hope that there is a life to come; they have sure, settled faith in it. This certainty transforms how they live. The person who truly believes in the Hereafter orders the present in light of the future, treating this life as a preparation, weighing choices by their eternal consequences, and refusing to sacrifice lasting good for temporary gain.\n\nThese three, prayer, charity, and faith in the Hereafter, form a powerful combination. Prayer keeps the heart connected to God. Charity keeps the hand connected to the needy. Faith in the Hereafter keeps the whole life oriented toward eternity. Together they describe a believer whose worship, conduct, and outlook are all rightly ordered.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a practical profile of the good-doer. It is not a vague or mystical description but a concrete set of practices and convictions that can be examined and cultivated. The verse invites us to ask, of each of the three, whether it is truly present and growing in our lives: a prayer life that is established, a practice of giving that is faithful, and a faith in the Hereafter that is sure. Where these three are found together, there is the life of a good-doer, the kind of life in which the guidance and mercy of the Book find their fullest expression.",
  "5": "“It is they who are truly guided by their Lord.” This verse pronounces a verdict on the people described in the previous verses, those who establish prayer, pay the alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter. They are “truly guided.” The word “truly” is emphatic. There are many kinds of guidance that people claim for themselves, philosophies, traditions, intuitions, but this verse identifies the genuinely guided. They are the ones whose guidance comes from their Lord, expressed in the concrete practices of faith.\n\nThe source of their guidance is “their Lord.” This is important. True guidance is not self-generated; it is received from God. The believers do not find their way by their own brilliance but by following the light their Lord has given. Their practices, the prayer, the charity, the faith, are the paths along which this divine guidance travels into their lives. By walking these paths, they position themselves to receive and embody the guidance that only God can give.\n\n“And it is they who will be successful.” The verse closes with a declaration about the outcome of such a life. The truly guided are the truly successful. This is a striking redefinition of success. The world often measures success by wealth, status, power, or fame. The Quran measures it by guidance, by being rightly related to God, by living in a way that leads to eternal good. The most outwardly impressive life, if it lacks this guidance, is ultimately a failure; the most modest life, if it is truly guided, is a success.\n\nThe connection between guidance and success is causal, not coincidental. Those who are guided by their Lord are led, step by step, toward the good for which they were made. Their guidance does not leave them stranded but carries them to their proper destination. Success is not a separate gift added to guidance; it is the natural fruit of being led by the One who knows where the path truly leads.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful encouragement and a clear criterion. The encouragement is that a life of prayer, charity, and faith in the Hereafter, however ordinary it may look from the outside, is a life of true guidance and certain success. The criterion is a redefinition of what matters: not the measures the world uses, but the measure of being truly guided by the Lord. The verse invites us to pursue this guidance above every other ambition, confident that those who walk in it are, in the only sense that ultimately matters, truly successful, led by their Lord to the good that will not fade.",
  "6": "“But there are some who employ theatrics, only to lead others away from Allah’s Way.” This verse turns from the good-doers to their opposites. Having described those who receive the Book’s guidance, the Quran now names those who actively work against it. There are people who “employ theatrics,” a phrase that suggests frivolous, distracting talk or entertainment, used not innocently but with a purpose: to lead others away from the path of God. Their words and amusements are weapons aimed at faith.\n\nThe phrase “without any knowledge” is important. Those who lead others astray in this way do so in ignorance. They have not examined the truth, weighed the evidence, or considered the consequences. They act out of whim, desire, or spite, spreading distraction and doubt without any solid basis for what they say or do. Their influence is real, but their authority is nil; they speak with confidence about things they do not understand.\n\n“And to make a mockery of it.” Their goal is twofold: to divert people from the Way of God, and to ridicule that Way. Mockery is a powerful tool, because it allows the mocker to dismiss something without ever engaging it seriously. By treating the faith as a joke, these people make it socially costly for others to take it seriously. The aim is to create an atmosphere in which faith is treated with contempt, and so to weaken it in the hearts of the vulnerable.\n\n“They will suffer a humiliating punishment.” The verse closes with the consequence for such behaviour. Those who use their influence to lead others away from God and to mock His Way will face a punishment described as “humiliating.” The word is significant. In this life, they may have felt clever, admired, even powerful, as their mockery entertained and their distraction captivated. In the Hereafter, all of that will be stripped away, and they will be left with nothing but shame. The very influence they abused will testify against them.\n\nThis verse sits in sharp contrast to the previous ones. Those described before were the guided, the successful, the good-doers. These are the misleaders, the mockers, the doomed. The two groups represent opposite responses to the truth and opposite destinies.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about how we use our words and our influence. It asks us to consider whether our speech leads people toward God or away from Him, whether we treat sacred things with reverence or with ridicule. The verse invites us to use our influence for good, to guard our tongues from mockery of the truth, and to remember that the way we speak about faith is not a small matter but one with serious, eternal consequences, both for those who hear us and for ourselves.",
  "7": "“Whenever Our revelations are recited to them, they turn away in arrogance.” This verse describes the response of the rejecters to the Quran itself. When God’s revelations are recited, these people do not listen, reflect, or consider. They turn away. And the cause of their turning is named: arrogance. It is not that the revelations are unclear or unconvincing; it is that the listeners are too proud to receive them. Arrogance builds a wall around the heart that the truth cannot penetrate.\n\nThe image of turning away “in arrogance” is powerful. It suggests a deliberate, contemptuous dismissal, a refusal even to grant the revelations a hearing. The proud person does not engage with the truth; he turns his back on it, treating it as beneath his attention. This stance is the opposite of the humility that the Quran’s guidance requires and rewards. The good-doers approach the Book with openness; the arrogant approach it with their backs turned.\n\n“As if they did not hear them, as if there is deafness in their ears.” The verse adds a vivid detail. The rejecters act as though they cannot hear the revelations at all, as though their ears are stopped up. Of course, their ears work perfectly well; the sounds reach them. But their hearts are deaf. They have made themselves unable to hear, not physically but spiritually. The revelation enters their ears but not their understanding, because their pride has shut the door between the two.\n\n“So give them good news, O Prophet, of a painful punishment.” The verse closes with an ironic command. The Prophet is told to give these people “good news,” but the news is of a painful punishment. The irony is sharp. They treated God’s revelations as nothing; now they are given news they cannot dismiss. The very message they refused to hear is replaced by a message of consequence that they will not be able to avoid.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the spiritual deafness that arrogance produces. The ability to hear God’s word is not guaranteed by functioning ears; it requires a humble heart. Pride, however subtle, can stop the ears and turn the back, leaving a person surrounded by revelation yet untouched by it. The verse invites us to examine our own posture toward God’s word: do we listen with humility, ready to be taught and changed, or do we turn away in arrogance, deaf to the very truth that could save us? The way we listen now will determine, the verse warns, whether we receive guidance or only the painful news of what our deafness has cost us.",
  "8": "“Surely those who believe and do good will have the Gardens of Bliss.” This verse returns to the promise of reward, balancing the warnings of the previous verses. After describing the rejecters and their painful punishment, the Quran now reaffirms the destiny of the believers. Those who combine faith with good action will have “the Gardens of Bliss.” The promise is introduced with “surely,” emphasising its certainty. This is not a vague hope but a firm assurance from God.\n\nThe “Gardens of Bliss” is a beautiful image for Paradise. Gardens suggest beauty, shade, fruit, water, and rest, everything that a person in a harsh land longs for. Bliss suggests a happiness that is deep, complete, and untroubled. The combination paints a picture of an existence in which every good thing is present and every sorrow is absent, a life of perfect contentment in a place of perfect beauty.\n\nThe description of the people who receive this reward is consistent with the whole Quran: “those who believe and do good.” Faith and action together. Neither alone is sufficient. Faith without good works is like a tree without fruit; good works without faith lack their deepest root. The Quran always joins the two, because the complete human life requires both: a heart turned toward God and hands engaged in goodness.\n\nThis verse connects to the opening of the chapter, which described the Book as guidance and mercy for the good-doers. The good-doers, who live by that guidance, are now shown their destination. The guidance that shapes their present leads them, step by step, to a future of bliss. The mercy that accompanies the guidance reaches its fullest expression in the Gardens promised to those who have walked the path.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a wellspring of hope and motivation. It assures us that the life of faith and good action, however much it may cost in this world, leads to a reward of surpassing beauty and joy. The Gardens of Bliss are real, and they belong to those who believe and do good. The verse invites us to keep believing, to keep doing good, even when the path is hard, with our eyes fixed on the destination, confident that the One who promised the Gardens is faithful, and that no act of sincere faith or goodness will go unrewarded in the end.",
  "9": "“Staying there forever.” These few words carry enormous weight. The reward of the believers is not a temporary pleasure that fades after a time. It is an eternal home, a dwelling in which they “stay forever.” After a lifetime of temporary enjoyments, passing comforts, and relationships that end, the believer arrives at something that does not end. The word “forever” answers the deepest ache of the human heart, the longing for something stable, something that cannot be taken away.\n\n“Allah’s promise is true.” The verse grounds the hope of Paradise in the trustworthiness of God’s promise. This is not a human aspiration that might disappoint; it is a divine promise, and God’s promises are always true. The believer can rest in absolute certainty, because the One who made the promise cannot lie and cannot fail. Whatever doubts may arise, whatever circumstances may seem to contradict the promise, the word of God stands sure.\n\n“And He is the Almighty, All-Wise.” The verse closes by naming two attributes that guarantee the promise. God is Almighty, possessing all power, which means He is fully able to do what He has promised. No obstacle can prevent the fulfilment of His word. And He is All-Wise, exercising that power with perfect wisdom, which means the promise is given and kept in a way that is perfectly good and right. The combination of power and wisdom ensures both that the promise will be kept and that its keeping will be flawless.\n\nThe structure of these two verses is a complete theology of hope. Verse eight names the reward, the Gardens of Bliss. This verse secures it, with the eternality of the dwelling, the truthfulness of the promise, and the power and wisdom of the Promiser. Nothing is left to chance. Every possible ground for doubt is removed, replaced by the solid foundations of God’s own character.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an anchor for the soul. In a world where everything passes, where every joy is shadowed by its coming end, the verse offers a hope that is eternal, a promise that is true, and a Promiser who is both almighty and all-wise. The verse invites us to rest our hearts in this hope, to let the certainty of an eternal home in the Gardens of Bliss sustain us through every loss and every trial of this passing life, knowing that the One who has promised is faithful, and that what He has prepared for those who love Him is as sure as His own unchanging nature.",
  "10": "“He created the heavens without pillars—as you can see.” This verse points to the heavens as a sign of God’s power, and it does so with a striking detail. The heavens, vast and towering above us, stand “without pillars.” There is no visible structure holding them up, no columns reaching from earth to sky. And yet they stand. The phrase “as you can see” is an invitation to look, to observe this marvel with one’s own eyes. The stability of the sky, which we take for granted, is itself a continuous miracle of divine power.\n\n“And placed firm mountains upon the earth so it does not shake with you.” The verse then turns to the earth and mentions the mountains. The mountains are described as stabilisers, fixed into the earth so that it does not shake and destabilise its inhabitants. Whether understood literally or as a broader image of the order that makes the earth liveable, the point is the same: the earth has been made stable and suitable for human life by God’s deliberate design. We did not make it so; we only benefit from it.\n\n“And scattered throughout it all types of creatures.” The verse marvels at the diversity of life spread across the earth. From the smallest insects to the largest animals, from creatures of the sea to beasts of the land, the earth teems with living things, each “scattered” through its proper habitat. This abundance and variety are presented as further signs of the Creator’s artistry and generosity.\n\n“And We send down rain from the sky, causing every type of fine plant to grow on earth.” The verse closes with the rain and the vegetation it produces, returning to the familiar image of water giving life. The rain descends, and from it springs up “every type of fine plant,” a rich variety of growth that sustains both human beings and the creatures. The whole cycle, water from sky, plants from earth, food for all, is the work of God, provided freely and abundantly.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to see the world as a coordinated system of divine signs. The heavens standing without pillars, the mountains stabilising the earth, the creatures scattered throughout, the rain producing plants, all of these are not isolated facts but parts of a single, purposeful creation. The verse invites us to look at the world around us and recognise in its order, stability, abundance, and beauty the hand of a wise and powerful Creator, who has made all of this not for His own need but as a gift for the creatures, including us, whom He has placed within it.",
  "11": "“This is Allah’s creation.” With this concise statement, the verse gathers together all the wonders described in the previous verse and attributes them to their true Author. The heavens, the earth, the mountains, the creatures, the rain, the plants, all of this is “Allah’s creation.” The verse does not present this as one possible explanation among many; it states it as the fact. The world around us is the handiwork of God, and recognising this is the beginning of true understanding.\n\n“Now show Me what those gods other than Him have created.” The verse then issues a challenge that exposes the emptiness of polytheism. If the idols and false gods are truly divine, let them produce some creation. Let them show even a small fraction of what Allah has made. The challenge is rhetorical, because the answer is obvious: the false gods have created nothing. They cannot make a heaven, a mountain, a creature, or a plant. They are themselves created things, powerless to create anything at all.\n\nThe contrast is devastating. On one side stands Allah, whose creation fills the heavens and the earth. On the other side stand the false gods, whose creation is precisely nothing. To worship beings who have created nothing, while ignoring the One who has created everything, is an act of staggering irrationality. The verse lays bare the absurdity of polytheism by a simple comparison of creative power.\n\n“In fact, the wrongdoers are clearly astray.” The verse closes with a verdict on those who worship these powerless gods. They are “clearly astray,” and their astrayal is not subtle or hidden but obvious. The evidence is all around them, in the creation that declares the one true Creator, and yet they turn from it to beings who can create nothing. Their error is plain to anyone who looks honestly.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear call to direct all worship to the One who has created all things. The logic is simple and powerful: worship belongs to the Creator, not to things that have themselves been created. The verse invites us to look at the world, recognise its true Author, and reserve for Him alone the reverence, gratitude, and devotion that His role as Creator so clearly deserves, refusing the obvious error of giving to powerless, created things the worship that belongs only to the One who made them and everything else besides.",
  "12": "“Indeed, We blessed Luqmân with wisdom.” With this verse, the chapter introduces its central figure, Luqmân, and identifies the source of the wisdom for which he is famous. Luqmân was not wise merely by natural talent or long experience; he was blessed with wisdom by God. Wisdom, in the Quranic understanding, is a gift, a divine bestowal that enables a person to see deeply, judge rightly, and live well. That God gave this gift to Luqmân is itself a sign of His generosity and of His care for guiding human beings through wise teachers.\n\n“Saying, Be grateful to Allah.” The content of the wisdom Luqmân received begins with gratitude. The very first instruction given to him, and so the foundation of his wisdom, is to be grateful to God. This is significant. Wisdom does not begin with complicated theories or rare knowledge; it begins with a simple, profound orientation of the heart toward the Giver of every good thing. Gratitude is the soil in which all other wisdom grows. The ungrateful heart, focused on what it lacks, cannot receive the deeper gifts; the grateful heart, aware of what it has been given, is open to ever-increasing light.\n\n“For whoever is grateful, it is only for their own good.” The verse then reveals the nature of gratitude: it benefits the one who is grateful. God is not enriched by our thanks; He needs nothing from us. Gratitude is for our own good, healing our hearts, ordering our desires, and opening us to further blessing. When we thank God, we are not giving Him something He lacks; we are receiving, into our own souls, the good that gratitude brings.\n\n“And whoever is ungrateful, then surely Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.” The verse closes by underlining God’s complete freedom from need. If a person is ungrateful, God loses nothing. He is Self-Sufficient, needing no creature’s thanks, and He is Praiseworthy, deserving of praise whether or not any tongue offers it. The ungrateful person harms only themselves, cutting themselves off from the good that gratitude would have brought. God remains, as always, complete in Himself, worthy of all praise from all creation.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to make gratitude the foundation of wisdom, as it was for Luqmân. The verse invites us to begin our own pursuit of wisdom not with the search for rare insights but with the simple, daily practice of thanking God for His gifts. Such gratitude, offered for our own good, opens the heart to receive the deeper wisdom God gives, and places us, like Luqmân, among those whom God has blessed with the ability to see deeply, judge rightly, and live well in a world full of His gifts.",
  "13": "“And remember when Luqmân said to his son, while advising him, O my dear son! Never associate anything with Allah in worship.” This verse begins the record of Luqmân’s advice to his son, and the very first piece of counsel he gives is the most important of all: never commit shirk, never associate anything with God in worship. The tenderness of the address, “O my dear son,” combined with the gravity of the instruction, shows both the love of a father and the seriousness of the truth. Luqmân begins where all true wisdom begins, with the absolute oneness of God and the demand for undivided worship.\n\nThe placement of this counsel first is significant. Before Luqmân speaks of prayer, of manners, of patience, of any other virtue, he establishes the foundation: worship God alone. Every other piece of advice rests on this. A life that gets this right can be rightly ordered in everything else; a life that gets this wrong is fundamentally disordered, no matter how many other virtues it may display. Tawhid, the oneness of God, is the root; all other wisdom is the branch.\n\n“For associating others with Him is truly the worst of all wrongs.” The verse gives the reason for the command, and it is a sobering one. Shirk is described as “the worst of all wrongs.” This is a startling statement. We might expect theft, murder, or cruelty to be named the worst wrongs. But the Quran identifies the association of partners with God as the supreme injustice, because it is a wrong done directly against the One who deserves all worship, the One who created and sustains everything. To give to a created thing the honour due to the Creator is the deepest possible disordering of reality.\n\nThe word “wrong” here carries the sense of injustice, of violating a right. God has the right to be worshipped alone, because He alone is the Creator, Sustainer, and Lord of all. To share that worship with anything else is to deny Him His right and to place something unworthy in His place. That is why it is the worst of wrongs: it strikes at the very foundation of truth and right relationship.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to examine the direction of our worship and trust. Luqmân’s first lesson to his son is the first lesson for us all: worship God alone, and refuse, absolutely, to give to anything else the love, reverence, and reliance that belong only to Him. The verse invites us to root out every form of shirk, whether obvious or subtle, from our hearts, recognising that this is not a minor matter but the worst of all wrongs, and that getting this right is the indispensable foundation of a wise and rightly ordered life.",
  "14": "“And We have commanded people to honour their parents.” Having established the worship of God alone as the foundation, Luqmân’s teaching, and the Quran’s, immediately turns to the treatment of parents. This pairing, the worship of God and the honouring of parents, appears throughout the Quran and reflects a deep wisdom: the One who gave us life through our parents is honoured, in part, through the way we treat those He used to bring us into being. Honour toward parents flows directly from, and is inseparable from, honour toward God.\n\n“Their mothers bore them through hardship upon hardship.” The verse singles out the mother for special mention, acknowledging the unique burdens of pregnancy and birth. The phrase “hardship upon hardship” captures the cumulative weight of carrying a child, the physical strain, the emotional investment, the vulnerability, and the pain of delivery. The Quran honours this sacrifice by naming it directly, calling the believer to remember what the mother endured and to respond with corresponding care and gratitude.\n\n“And their weaning takes two years.” The verse mentions the period of weaning, generally understood as about two years, during which the infant is wholly dependent on the mother. This period of total dependence is recalled to deepen the sense of obligation. The parents did not merely bring the child into the world; they sustained the child through years of complete helplessness, feeding, protecting, and nurturing at great cost to themselves.\n\n“So be grateful to Me and your parents.” The verse then commands gratitude, directed toward two objects: toward God, the ultimate Giver of life and all things, and toward the parents, the immediate means of that gift. The two gratitudes are joined, because they are related. The grateful heart thanks God through thanking the people He used to bless it, and thanks the parents while recognising that every good, including the parents themselves, ultimately comes from God.\n\n“To Me is the final return.” The verse closes with a reminder of the ultimate accountability. The final return is to God, and at that return, the way we treated our parents will be among the things examined. Honouring parents is not merely a social nicety; it is a religious duty with eternal significance, recorded and reckoned on the Day when we return to our Lord.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to a lifelong posture of honour and gratitude toward parents, especially toward the mother, whose hardships the verse so tenderly acknowledges. It invites us to remember what our parents endured for us, to thank God for them, to treat them with kindness and respect, and to live in the awareness that the way we honour them is bound up with the way we honour the God who commanded such honour and to whom, in the end, we all return.",
  "15": "“But if they pressure you to associate with Me what you have no knowledge of, do not obey them.” This verse establishes the crucial limit on the duty to parents, and it is the same limit stated elsewhere in the Quran. As high as the command to honour parents is, it does not extend to disobedience of God. If parents pressure a person to commit shirk, to associate partners with the Creator, then obedience stops. The believer remains respectful but does not comply. Loyalty to God stands above every other loyalty, even the deepest human bond.\n\n“Still keep their company in this world courteously.” This is the beautiful balance of the Quran. Even when parents are pressuring their child toward wrong, the child is commanded to keep their company “courteously.” The refusal to obey in matters of faith does not license rudeness, coldness, or abandonment. The believer continues to treat the parents with kindness, patience, and good character. The disagreement is real, but it is carried with grace. This is a high standard, and it protects both the truth of faith and the dignity of the parental relationship.\n\n“And follow the way of those who turn to Me in devotion.” The verse then offers positive direction. Having refused the parents’ pressure toward shirk, the believer is to follow a different path: the way of those who turn to God in devotion. The believer needs a community, a model, a way of life to follow, and that way is the way of the devoted. By walking with those who have turned to God, the believer finds strength to resist wrong pressure and a pattern for living rightly.\n\n“Then to Me you will all return, and then I will inform you of what you used to do.” The verse closes by lifting the reader’s eyes to the Day of Judgment. All, parents and children alike, will return to God, and then the truth of every choice will be revealed, including how the believer balanced the duty to parents against the duty to the Creator. The verse holds out both the certainty of accountability and, by implication, the reward for those who navigated this difficult balance faithfully.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a wise and compassionate guide to one of life’s most painful tensions. It refuses to force a choice between loving parents and loving God, showing instead how both can be honoured even when they seem to conflict: by refusing compromise on the worship of God alone, by maintaining courtesy and kindness toward parents regardless, and by walking in the company of the devoted. The verse invites us to live with this balance, trusting that the God who commanded both duties will, on the Day of return, recognise and reward the faithfulness that held them together with truth and grace.",
  "16": "“Luqmân added, O my dear son! Even if a deed were the weight of a mustard seed—be it hidden in a rock or in the heavens or the earth—Allah will bring it forth.” This verse records a remarkable piece of Luqmân’s wisdom, addressed again with tender love to “my dear son.” The counsel concerns the absolute thoroughness of divine accountability. Even a deed as tiny as a mustard seed, the smallest thing his son could imagine, hidden in the most inaccessible place, whether deep in a rock, high in the heavens, or buried in the earth, will be brought forth by God. Nothing is too small to be noticed; nothing is too hidden to be found.\n\nThe image of the mustard seed is chosen for its tininess. A mustard seed is among the smallest things a person in that world would handle. Luqmân uses it to make a point about God’s precision: the divine accounting is not approximate or coarse. It does not overlook the little things. Every deed, however minor, is recorded and will be brought to light. This should make the believer both careful and hopeful: careful, because no wrong is too small to matter; hopeful, because no good is too small to be rewarded.\n\nThe places named, a rock, the heavens, the earth, represent every possible hiding place. A deed concealed in solid rock, as if forever inaccessible; a deed hidden in the vast heavens; a deed buried in the depths of the earth; none of these can escape God’s knowledge. The verse affirms God’s complete access to every corner of creation. There is no place beyond His reach, no secret beyond His knowledge.\n\n“Surely Allah is Most Subtle, All-Aware.” The verse closes with two of God’s names that ground the teaching. He is Most Subtle, the Arabic name carrying the sense of One who works in fine, hidden, delicate ways, perceiving the minutest realities. And He is All-Aware, fully informed of every detail, inward and outward. The combination assures us that God’s accounting is both infinitely precise and infinitely comprehensive.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to live with constant awareness of God’s perfect knowledge. Every deed, every word, every hidden intention, down to the weight of a mustard seed, is seen and will be brought forth. This awareness, rather than being oppressive, is deeply purifying. It invites us to be faithful in the smallest things, knowing that nothing good is lost and nothing wrong is forgotten, and to rest in the care of a Lord who is Most Subtle and All-Aware, whose knowledge of us is complete, whose justice is perfect, and whose recording of every mustard seed of a deed is the guarantee that, in the end, everything will be exactly as it should be.",
  "17": "“O my dear son! Establish prayer, encourage what is good and forbid what is evil, and endure patiently whatever befalls you.” This verse records another strand of Luqmân’s counsel, and it gathers three of the most central practices of a life of faith. The first is the establishment of prayer, the regular, devoted worship that keeps the heart connected to God. The second is the active engagement with good and evil in society: encouraging what is good and forbidding what is evil. The third is patience, the steadfast endurance that holds firm through whatever comes.\n\nThe order is significant. Prayer comes first, because the relationship with God is the foundation. From that relationship flows the strength and clarity to engage with the world, encouraging good and discouraging evil. And undergirding both the worship and the social engagement is patience, because both are costly and both require endurance. The three form a complete life: devotion to God, responsibility to society, and the inner strength to sustain both.\n\n“Encourage what is good and forbid what is evil” is one of the great communal duties in Islam. The believer is not meant to be passive, living a private goodness while the wider community decays. Rather, the believer is to be active, promoting virtue and resisting vice, using wisdom, kindness, and courage to call others toward good and away from harm. This duty links personal faith with public responsibility, reminding us that we are our brothers’ and sisters’ keepers.\n\n“Endure patiently whatever befalls you.” Patience is named last but is essential, because both prayer and the enjoining of good are difficult. Prayer requires discipline and persistence; encouraging good and forbidding evil often brings opposition, misunderstanding, and hardship. Without patience, the believer will grow weary and give up. With patience, the believer endures, trusting that the difficulties are part of the path and that the reward is with God.\n\n“Surely this is a resolve to aspire to.” The verse closes by honouring these practices as a mark of true resolve. The Arabic word carries the sense of a firm, determined purpose, something that requires real strength of character. Luqmân is telling his son that this combination of prayer, social responsibility, and patience is not for the faint-hearted; it is a high calling, worthy of the strongest aspiration.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a compact summary of the active life of faith, passed from a wise father to his son and now to us all. It invites us to establish our prayer, to take responsibility for the good of our communities, and to cultivate the patience that sustains both, recognising that this threefold life is a noble resolve, worthy of our highest aspiration, and the path by which a believer honours God, serves others, and grows strong enough to endure whatever the journey requires.",
  "18": "“And do not turn your nose up to people, nor walk pridefully upon the earth.” This verse records Luqmân’s counsel about manners and humility, and it addresses two common expressions of arrogance. The first is “turning your nose up,” a vivid image of contempt, of looking down on others with disdain. The second is walking “pridefully,” strutting through the world with an exaggerated sense of one’s own importance. Both behaviours are condemned, because both spring from the same root: a heart inflated with pride.\n\nThe image of turning up the nose captures the subtle cruelty of contempt. It is possible to communicate disdain without a word, simply through a look, a gesture, a posture. Luqmân warns his son against this quiet arrogance, this treating of other people as beneath him. Every human being is a creature of God, deserving of basic dignity and respect. To look down on anyone is to forget that we are all, ourselves, humble creatures before our Maker.\n\nThe warning against walking pridefully addresses the way a person carries themselves in the world. Pride shows itself in gait, in bearing, in the whole manner of moving through life. The proud person takes up space as though the world revolves around them, demanding attention and deference. Luqmân counsels a different bearing: one of humility, of walking the earth as a servant of God, not as its master.\n\n“Surely Allah does not like whoever is arrogant, boastful.” The verse closes by naming the divine response to pride. God does not like the arrogant. This is a serious statement, because the approval of God is the most valuable thing a person can have. To be disliked by God because of arrogance is a terrible price to pay for a moment of self-importance. The verse links the small, daily expressions of pride, the turned-up nose, the boastful walk, with the large, eternal reality of God’s displeasure.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to cultivate humility in both heart and manner. It invites us to examine how we look at others and how we carry ourselves, rooting out the contempt and the self-importance that pride breeds. The verse reminds us that humility is not weakness but wisdom, the accurate recognition that we are creatures among creatures, all equally dependent on the One Creator. And it warns us that arrogance, however satisfying it may feel in the moment, costs us the one thing we cannot afford to lose: the good pleasure of the God who does not like the proud.",
  "19": "“Be moderate in your pace.” This verse continues Luqmân’s counsel about conduct, turning now to the way a person moves and speaks. The first instruction is moderation in pace. The believer is not to rush about frantically, as though everything depended on haste, nor to move with the sluggishness of apathy. Moderation suggests a calm, deliberate, dignified way of going about one’s business, neither harried nor lazy. The way a person walks reflects the state of their heart, and a moderate pace reflects a heart at peace, trusting in God’s provision of time and outcome.\n\n“And lower your voice, for the ugliest of all voices is certainly the braying of donkeys.” The second instruction concerns the voice. Luqmân counsels his son to lower his voice, to speak with restraint rather than loudness. To make his point memorable, he uses a striking comparison: the ugliest of all voices is the braying of a donkey. The image is vivid and slightly humorous, but its lesson is serious. A loud, harsh, attention-seeking voice is displeasing, both to people and, by implication, to the standards of dignified conduct that faith encourages.\n\nThe comparison to a donkey’s bray is chosen for its unpleasantness. The bray is loud, grating, and without purpose or beauty. Luqmân uses it to show his son that raising one’s voice aggressively is not a sign of strength but a kind of ugliness. True strength and dignity are expressed through measured, respectful speech, not through volume. The believer who wishes to be heard and respected does not need to shout; they need to speak with wisdom, kindness, and self-control.\n\nThese two instructions, about pace and voice, complete a portrait of the believer’s outward manner that matches the inward virtues Luqmân has been teaching. Having counselled faith, prayer, patience, and humility, he now shows how these inner qualities express themselves in everyday behaviour: in a calm walk and a gentle voice. The inner and the outer are consistent. A humble heart produces a modest pace and a lowered voice.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that faith is expressed in the small details of daily conduct, not only in grand acts of worship. The way we walk and the way we speak are part of our witness. The verse invites us to cultivate moderation and restraint in our pace and our voice, letting the peace and humility of our hearts show through in our manner, and avoiding the frantic haste and the loud self-assertion that the world often mistakes for strength but that Luqmân, in his wisdom, identifies as the marks of a character that has not yet learned true dignity.",
  "20": "“Have you not seen that Allah has subjected for you whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.” This verse calls attention to one of the greatest signs of God’s generosity: the subjection of creation to human use. Everything in the heavens and on the earth, the sun, the moon, the stars, the rain, the animals, the plants, the seas, has been made subservient to human beings. We did not create these things; we did not place them at our service. God subjected them to us, arranging the whole cosmos so that it would sustain and benefit the human creature.\n\nThe word “subjected” is important. It means that creation has been placed under human authority, not as a tyrant’s dominion but as a steward’s trust. The sun shines for us, the rain falls for us, the animals serve us, the plants feed us, all by God’s arrangement. This vast, coordinated system, tuned so precisely to human need, is presented as a sign of God’s care. The universe is not indifferent to us; it has been ordered for our benefit by a wise and generous Creator.\n\n“And has lavished His favours upon you, both seen and unseen.” The verse adds that God’s favours are “lavished,” not measured out sparingly but poured out abundantly. And they come in two kinds: seen and unseen. The seen favours are obvious: food, health, family, shelter. The unseen favours are countless and often unrecognised: the dangers averted without our knowledge, the hardships eased before we felt them, the blessings of guidance and faith, the workings of providence that sustain us moment by moment. The combination of seen and unseen means that God’s generosity surrounds us completely, whether we perceive it or not.\n\n“Still there are some who dispute about Allah without knowledge, or guidance, or an enlightening scripture.” The verse ends with a note of astonishment at human ingratitude. Despite the overwhelming evidence of God’s generosity, some people still dispute about Him, and they do so “without knowledge, or guidance, or an enlightening scripture.” Their arguments have no basis, no foundation in truth. They dispute against the very God who has lavished every good thing upon them, and they do so in ignorance, lacking the knowledge that would set them right.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to wonder and gratitude. It invites us to look at the world and recognise how much has been subjected to our use, how many favours, seen and unseen, have been lavished upon us. Such recognition should fill the heart with thanksgiving and silence the impulse to dispute about God. The verse invites us to receive God’s gifts with gratitude, to seek the knowledge and guidance that some lack, and to live as people who have seen, in the subjection of creation and the lavishing of favours, more than enough evidence to fill a lifetime with praise.",
  "21": "“When it is said to them, Follow what Allah has revealed, they reply, No! We only follow what we found our forefathers practicing.” This verse records a common and stubborn response to the call of truth. When people are invited to follow what God has revealed, they refuse, citing tradition as their authority. They will follow only what they found their forefathers practicing. The answer sounds respectful, as though it honours the ancestors, but it is in fact a refusal to think, a preference for inherited habit over revealed guidance.\n\nThe appeal to ancestral custom is one of the oldest obstacles to truth. It is comfortable, because it requires no examination; it is socially powerful, because it aligns with the community; and it feels virtuous, because it seems to respect one’s heritage. But the Quran exposes its weakness. Tradition is not, by itself, a guide to truth. If the forefathers were wrong, then following them simply perpetuates the error. The value of a practice lies not in its age but in its truth, and truth is established by revelation and reason, not by mere inheritance.\n\n“Would they still do so even if Satan is inviting them to the torment of the Blaze?” The verse then asks a searching question that reframes the whole issue. The appeal to tradition is not innocent. Behind it may lie a far darker reality: the invitation of Satan to destruction. The verse suggests that clinging to inherited falsehood can, in fact, be a way of following Satan, who delights to see human beings refuse God’s guidance and persist in paths that lead to ruin. The contrast between honouring forefathers and being led by Satan to the Blaze is deliberately shocking. What feels like loyalty to tradition may, in truth, be a march toward destruction.\n\nThe word “even if” is crucial. It asks whether the attachment to tradition is so strong that a person would persist in it knowing that it leads, through Satan’s invitation, to the Fire. Put that way, the absurdity of the attachment becomes clear. No loyalty to custom is worth the torment of the Blaze. Yet people cling to inherited ways precisely because they do not pause to consider where those ways actually lead.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a challenge to examine the basis of our beliefs and practices. It does not ask us to dishonour our forefathers, but it asks us to test what we have inherited against what God has revealed. Tradition is not a safe substitute for truth. The verse invites us to follow what Allah has revealed, even when it requires us to part from inherited ways, and to recognise that genuine respect for our heritage is best expressed not by blind imitation but by seeking and following the truth, the only path that truly honours both our forefathers and the God who created us all.",
  "22": "“Whoever fully submits themselves to Allah and is a good-doer, they have certainly grasped the firmest hand-hold.” This verse describes the person who has found the true and secure path, and it does so with a powerful image. The one who submits fully to God, combining inward surrender with outward goodness, has “grasped the firmest hand-hold.” The image suggests a grip that cannot slip, a hold that cannot fail. In a world full of uncertainties and shifting foundations, this person has laid hold of something utterly secure.\n\nThe “firmest hand-hold” is, in essence, Islam itself: the submission to God that the whole Quran calls for. But it is described here not as an abstract belief but as a grasp, an active clinging. The believer does not merely assent to submission; they hold onto it, rely on it, and refuse to let go. And because the thing held is God Himself, the grip is sure. Other hand-holds, wealth, status, human approval, false worship, may slip or break; the hand-hold of submission to God never does.\n\nThe combination in the verse is important: full submission AND good-doing. The two together form the complete picture of the believer. Submission without good works is incomplete; good works without submission lack their deepest meaning. Joined together, they constitute the life that has grasped the firmest hand-hold, the life that is both rightly related to God and rightly engaged with the world.\n\n“And with Allah rests the outcome of all affairs.” The verse closes with a statement that grounds the security of the believer. The outcome, the final result, of every affair rests with God. Whatever appears uncertain in the middle of the story is settled in God’s hands at the end. This is the ultimate reason the believer’s grip is firm: not because the believer is strong, but because the One they hold onto controls every outcome. The believer can trust the future to God, knowing that all affairs, including their own, will reach their proper conclusion in His perfect wisdom.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is both an invitation and an assurance. The invitation is to grasp the firmest hand-hold: to submit fully to God and to do good, holding tight to the faith that alone offers true security. The assurance is that the outcome of all things rests with God, so the believer need not fear. The verse invites us to commit ourselves wholeheartedly to this grasp, to live as people whose grip is sure because of what they hold, and to rest in the knowledge that, whatever the present may look like, the final outcome of every affair belongs to the One whom we have taken hold of and who, in His mercy, has taken hold of us.",
  "23": "“To Us is their return, and We will inform them of all they did.” The previous verses ended by celebrating the one who has grasped the firmest hand-hold of faith. This verse now turns to those who refuse that hand-hold, the disbelievers, and it gives the Prophet a steadying word about them. He is not to let their disbelief grieve him. Their rejection is painful, especially when it comes from people he longs to guide, but he is reminded that the matter does not end with their refusal. There is a return, and at that return every deed will be brought into the open.\n\nThe instruction not to grieve is not a call to coldness. The Prophet cared deeply about his people; the Quran elsewhere describes his near-grief over their rejection. Rather, the verse consoles him by lifting his eyes to the justice that is coming. The disbelievers may seem, for the moment, to get away with their denial, but they are heading toward a meeting where nothing will be hidden. Grief over their present refusal is eased by certainty about their future accounting.\n\n“We will inform them of all they did.” The accounting will be complete. Every act, open and secret, will be laid before them. This is both justice and exposure. The disbeliever who imagined that denial carried no cost will discover that every choice was recorded, and that the record is now being read back. There is no escaping one’s own deeds, because the One who records is the One to whom all return.\n\n“Surely Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart.” The verse closes by affirming God’s perfect knowledge of the inner life. The deeds that will be informed are matched by the secrets that are already known. God does not need the Day of Judgment to learn what is in the heart; He knows it now, completely. The judgment simply makes that knowledge visible to the one being judged.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a double comfort and a double warning. It comforts the believer who grieves over the rejection of others, assuring them that justice is certain and the outcome is in God’s hands. And it warns the disbeliever that denial is not the end of the story, that a return is coming, and that the One who knows the secrets of every heart will, in the end, inform each soul of exactly what it did, leaving no deed unrecorded and no heart unexamined.",
  "24": "“We allow them enjoyment for a little while.” This verse speaks about the disbelievers’ present condition and exposes a common misunderstanding about worldly success. The prosperity and ease that the wicked sometimes enjoy is not a sign of God’s approval. It is, the verse says, a brief allowance, a short season of enjoyment that will come to an end. The disbeliever may look comfortable now, but the comfort is on loan, and the loan has a due date.\n\nThe phrase “for a little while” puts all worldly pleasure in its true perspective. A lifetime of ease, however long it feels while it lasts, is “a little while” measured against eternity. The disbeliever who builds a life of enjoyment on the foundation of denial is building something temporary, and the brevity of the enjoyment is itself a mercy in disguise, a limited time after which the real accounting begins. The enjoyment is real but short; what follows is what truly matters.\n\n“Then, in time, We will force them into a harsh torment.” The verse then names the consequence that follows the enjoyment. The word “force” is sobering. The disbelievers will not walk willingly into the torment; they will be driven into it. Every escape will be closed, every refuge removed, and the reality they spent their lives denying will overtake them. The transition from “enjoyment” to “harsh torment” is deliberate and complete, the starkest possible contrast.\n\nThe pairing of these two halves teaches a profound lesson about the nature of this world as a place of testing. The enjoyment is given, in part, as a respite and a test: what will a person do with ease, with comfort, with provision? Will they turn grateful, or will they grow arrogant and forget the Giver? The disbeliever uses the enjoyment to forget, and so the enjoyment itself becomes, in the end, a witness against them, a mercy they abused and a time they wasted.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to view worldly comfort with clear eyes. Ease is not proof of divine favour, and hardship is not proof of divine anger. Both are tests, brief seasons in a life that is itself brief. The verse invites us to use whatever enjoyment we are given with gratitude and remembrance, rather than letting it lull us into heedlessness, and to live in the awareness that the “little while” of this world will give way, inevitably, to the reality that follows, where the only thing that will matter is how we used the time we were lent.",
  "25": "“And if you ask them who created the heavens and the earth, they will definitely say, Allah!” This verse records a striking fact about the idolaters. If you asked them directly who made the heavens and the earth, they would answer, “Allah.” Even in their polytheism, they acknowledged that the One true God was the Creator. Their idols were not understood as makers of the universe; they were approached as intermediaries or lesser powers. But when pressed on the ultimate question of creation, the idolaters admitted what every honest soul knows: Allah is the Creator of everything.\n\nThis admission is the foundation of the Quran’s argument against them. The same confession that rises naturally from their lips condemns their worship. If you know that Allah alone created the heavens and the earth, then He alone deserves your worship. To direct devotion elsewhere while acknowledging God as the Creator is a contradiction that the verse exposes with surgical clarity.\n\n“Say, Praise be to Allah!” The Prophet is told to respond with praise. Since the truth is confessed even by those who stray from it, the fitting response is gratitude to the One who is, by their own admission, the Creator of all. The praise is both worship and witness, honouring God while quietly underlining the absurdity of giving worship to anything else.\n\n“In fact, most of them do not know.” The verse closes with a sober explanation. Although the words “Allah” are on their lips, most of them do not truly know. They say the right thing without understanding its implications. They confess the Creator with their tongues but fail to follow the confession to its logical conclusion: wholehearted worship of the One they have named. Their knowledge is shallow, a formula repeated without its meaning being grasped.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to follow truth to its conclusion. It is not enough to know that God is the Creator; that knowledge must shape how we live, what we worship, and where we place our trust. The verse challenges the half-knowledge that confesses God with the mouth while the heart wanders after other loyalties, and it calls us to a knowing that is complete, a recognition of the Creator so deep and sincere that it flows naturally into a life of praise, gratitude, and undivided worship.",
  "26": "“To Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth.” This verse states the comprehensive ownership of God in the plainest possible terms. Everything in the heavens and the earth, the angels, the stars, the planets, the human beings, the animals, the unseen realms, all of it belongs to Allah. There is nothing in existence that falls outside His ownership. The universe is not a patchwork of independent domains; it is a single estate, and its Owner is God.\n\nThe significance of this truth is vast. If everything belongs to God, then nothing truly belongs to anyone else. The wealth a person holds is held in trust, not owned outright. The power a person exercises is delegated, not absolute. The life a person lives is a gift, not a possession. Recognising that all belongs to God transforms how a person relates to everything: with humility rather than pride, with gratitude rather than entitlement, with stewardship rather than exploitation.\n\n“Surely Allah is truly the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.” The verse closes with two of God’s attributes that flow directly from His ownership. He is Self-Sufficient, needing nothing from any of His creation. He does not own the heavens and the earth because He needs them; they add nothing to His perfection. And He is Praiseworthy, deserving of all praise whether or not any tongue offers it. His ownership is not the ownership of a needy landlord but of a Lord whose wealth is infinite and whose worth is beyond measure.\n\nThe pairing of these two attributes is significant. God’s self-sufficiency means that our worship and service bring Him no gain; He is complete without them. His praiseworthiness means that our worship is still our duty and our joy, because He deserves it regardless of whether He needs it. We worship not to enrich God but because He is worthy, and in worshipping we find our own fulfilment, for the creature is never more truly itself than when it acknowledges the One to whom all things belong.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to live as a steward rather than an owner. Everything we have, our wealth, our talents, our time, our very lives, belongs to God and is held by us in trust. The verse invites us to handle everything with that awareness: to use what we have been given as the Owner would have it used, to hold it loosely, to give from it generously, and to live in constant gratitude toward the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy Lord, from whom all things have come and to whom all things belong.",
  "27": "“If all the trees on earth were pens and the ocean were ink, refilled by seven other oceans, the Words of Allah would not be exhausted.” This verse paints one of the most magnificent images in the Quran to convey the limitless nature of God’s knowledge and speech. Imagine every tree on earth turned into a pen, and the entire ocean turned into ink, and then imagine that ocean refilled seven times over by seven more oceans. Even with such an unimaginable supply of writing materials, the “Words of Allah” would not run out. His knowledge and speech are inexhaustible.\n\nThe image works by overwhelming the imagination. The trees of the earth are beyond counting; the oceans are beyond measuring. To multiply the oceans sevenfold is to push the picture to the edge of what the mind can hold. And yet all of this vast supply would be insufficient to exhaust the Words of God. The point is not to be taken as a literal calculation but as a powerful statement: God’s knowledge, wisdom, and speech have no limit, no bottom, no end.\n\nThe “Words of Allah” can be understood in several complementary senses. They include God’s speech as recorded in revelation, the truths and laws and guidance He has given. They include His words of creation, by which He called all things into being. And they include His knowledge of every detail of existence, past, present, and future. In every sense, His words are beyond exhaustion, because the One who speaks them is infinite.\n\n“Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” The verse closes by grounding the image in two of God’s attributes. He is Almighty, possessing power without limit, which is why His words and works are inexhaustible. And He is All-Wise, exercising that power with perfect wisdom, so that every word He speaks is full of meaning and purpose. The limitless words are not empty quantity but infinite depth of wisdom.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to wonder and humility. The God we worship is not a limited being whose knowledge could be written down or whose wisdom could be exhausted. He is the One whose words would fill the oceans and still not run dry. Such a vision of God should fill us with awe, deepen our reverence for His revelation, and make us approach His words with the expectation of finding ever more, knowing that no lifetime of study could ever reach the bottom of what the Almighty, All-Wise Lord has spoken.",
  "28": "“The creation and resurrection of you all is as simple for Him as that of a single soul.” This verse states a profound truth about God’s power in a single, striking comparison. The creation and resurrection of all humanity, the countless billions who have ever lived and ever will live, is “as simple” for God as the creation and resurrection of a single soul. The difference that looms so large in our eyes, between one and multitudes, vanishes entirely before the power of God.\n\nThe comparison is meant to dismantle a common objection to resurrection. Human beings find it hard to believe that God could raise all the dead at once, because to us, creating many is harder than creating one. We project our own limitations onto God. But the verse insists that this reasoning does not apply to the Almighty. For God, the effort of creating and resurrecting one soul and the effort of creating and resurrecting all souls are the same, because He is not strained by either. His power does not diminish with use, nor does it grow tired with scale.\n\nThis teaching has deep pastoral implications. It means that no one is overlooked in the crowd. The believer might fear being lost among the multitudes, a single soul among countless others, but the verse assures us that God’s attention and power are not divided. The same ease with which He deals with one soul, He deals with all. Each person is known, created, sustained, and will be raised, with the same perfect attention.\n\n“Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.” The verse closes with two attributes that reinforce the message. He is All-Hearing, so not a word of any soul escapes Him. And He is All-Seeing, so not a deed is hidden from His sight. The One for whom multitudes are as simple as a single soul is also the One who hears and sees each of those souls, individually and completely. His power is matched by His awareness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a comfort and an assurance. We are not lost in the crowd before God. The same Lord who will raise all of humanity on the Day of Resurrection knows each of us, hears us, and sees us, with a completeness that no human attention could ever match. The verse invites us to live in the awareness of this all-seeing, all-hearing God, confident that the One for whom our resurrection is “as simple” as a single breath is also the One who knows us more deeply than we know ourselves.",
  "29": "“Do you not see that Allah causes the night to merge into the day and the day into the night?” This verse points to one of the most familiar and yet most marvelous phenomena of the natural world: the gradual transition between night and day. The verse describes this transition as a “merging,” and the image is exact. The night does not simply end and the day begin; rather, the two merge into one another, the darkness slowly giving way to light at dawn, the light slowly fading into darkness at dusk. This smooth, continuous transformation is presented as a sign of God’s wisdom and power.\n\nThe merging of night and day reveals a deep orderliness in creation. The transition is not abrupt but gentle, suited to the needs of living things. Eyes are given time to adjust, the temperature shifts gradually, and the rhythms of life can move smoothly from rest to activity and back. Such delicate design points to a Designer who cares about the comfort and flourishing of His creatures.\n\n“And has subjected the sun and the moon, each orbiting for an appointed term.” The verse then turns to the sun and moon, describing them as “subjected,” placed in service by God’s command. Each follows its course, orbiting for an “appointed term,” a fixed period determined by divine decree. The regularity of the heavens, the sunrise and sunset, the phases of the moon, the cycle of the year, all of this is the result of a precise, God-ordained order. The sun and moon do not wander at random; they move on schedule, because they have been set in their paths by the One who appointed their terms.\n\n“And that Allah is All-Aware of what you do?” The verse closes by connecting the grand order of the cosmos to the personal awareness of God. The same Lord who merges night into day and directs the sun and moon is also fully aware of what each human being does. The vastness of God’s cosmic governance does not distract Him from the details of human conduct; on the contrary, the same power and wisdom that order the heavens also watch over every deed.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to see the world as a single, coordinated sign. The dawn, the dusk, the sun’s journey, the moon’s phases, all are continuous displays of God’s wisdom, and all are matched by His awareness of us. The verse invites us to live with a sense of being both small before the cosmos and known within it, watched over by the same Lord who orders the heavens, and so to conduct ourselves always in the awareness that the All-Aware God sees not only the merging of night and day but every act of every soul beneath them.",
  "30": "“That is because Allah alone is the Truth and what they invoke besides Him is falsehood.” This verse draws the great dividing line between God and everything else. Allah “alone is the Truth,” the word here carrying the sense of the truly real, the genuinely existent, the one whose being is necessary and enduring. Everything else, by comparison, is contingent and dependent. And what people invoke besides Him, the idols, the false gods, the powers they raise to the level of worship, is “falsehood,” lacking the reality they attribute to it.\n\nThe contrast between truth and falsehood here is not merely a difference of opinion. It is a difference of being. God is real in the fullest sense, self-existent, eternal, the source of all that is. The false gods have no such reality. They are inventions, projections, created things treated as uncreated. To invoke them is to direct one’s devotion toward nothing, toward a shadow mistaken for substance. The verse exposes the tragedy of polytheism: the worshipper reaches out for something real and grasps only emptiness.\n\n“And because Allah alone is the Most High, All-Great.” The verse closes with two attributes that establish God’s absolute supremacy. He is the Most High, exalted above everything, answerable to none. And He is All-Great, possessing a greatness that nothing can rival or diminish. These attributes leave no room for partners. A God who is Most High and All-Great cannot share His worship with beings who are neither high nor great. The uniqueness of His being demands the uniqueness of His worship.\n\nThe structure of the verse connects the previous signs to this conclusion. The merging of night and day, the subjecting of the sun and moon, all of these display the truth and greatness of God. From such overwhelming evidence of one supreme Creator, the verse draws the only reasonable conclusion: He alone is the Truth, and everything invoked besides Him is falsehood.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to examine the direction of our devotion. Are we reaching toward the Truth, the one real and enduring God, or toward falsehoods that promise much and deliver nothing? The verse invites us to direct all our worship, trust, and love to the One who alone is Most High and All-Great, and to release our grip on the empty things that can never sustain the weight of a human heart, recognising that only the Truth can satisfy the soul that was made for truth.",
  "31": "“Do you not see that the ships sail smoothly through the sea by the grace of Allah?” This verse points to another sign of God’s mercy and power: the sailing of ships across the sea. A ship is a human-built vessel, yet its ability to move safely over the water depends entirely on laws and forces established by God, the wind, the buoyancy of water, the currents. The verse attributes the ships’ smooth sailing to “the grace of Allah,” reminding us that even human industry rests on divine provision. We build the ships, but God made the sea that carries them and the wind that drives them.\n\n“So that He may show you some of His signs.” The purpose of this, and of all the ordered processes of the world, is to display God’s signs. The sailing of a ship is not merely a practical matter of transport; it is a sign, a visible reminder of the Creator’s wisdom and care. Every smooth voyage, every cargo safely delivered, every traveller who reaches a distant shore, is a small testimony to the God who made such things possible. The verse invites us to see the ordinary as full of meaning.\n\n“Surely in this are signs for whoever is steadfast, grateful.” The verse closes by naming the kind of person who reads these signs: one who is steadfast and grateful. Steadfastness, the patient endurance that holds firm through life’s trials, and gratitude, the thankful recognition of God’s gifts, are the two qualities that open the eyes to see God’s signs. The heedless pass by the sailing ships and see only commerce; the steadfast and grateful see, in the same ships, the grace of a merciful Lord.\n\nThe connection between steadfastness, gratitude, and the reading of signs is significant. A heart trained by patience through hardship and by thankfulness in blessing is a heart that has been softened and sharpened to perceive spiritual reality. Such a heart finds signs everywhere, in the sea no less than in the sky. The impatient and the ungrateful, by contrast, are too distracted or too hardened to notice what is being shown them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to cultivate the qualities that make us able to read the world. The next time you watch a ship, or reflect on any human undertaking that succeeds through the ordering of nature, the verse invites you to see it as a sign of God’s grace, and to respond with the steadfastness and gratitude that turn ordinary observation into worship, recognising in the smooth sailing of a vessel the steady hand of the One who made the sea and everything in it.",
  "32": "“And as soon as they are overwhelmed by waves like mountains, they cry out to Allah alone in sincere devotion.” This verse returns to a theme seen elsewhere in the Quran: the instinctive turn of the human heart to God in moments of genuine danger. When sailors are caught in a storm, when the waves rise “like mountains” around them and death seems certain, the polytheism they practiced in calm weather evaporates. They cry out to Allah alone, with complete sincerity, because in the face of real catastrophe, the heart knows that only the One true God can save.\n\nThe image of waves like mountains conveys the overwhelming scale of the danger. Beside such waves, a human being is utterly powerless. All the false securities, wealth, status, the intercession of idols, vanish in an instant. What remains is the naked cry of the creature to its Creator. The verse exposes the truth that polytheism is a luxury of calm weather; in the storm, every soul knows to whom it truly belongs and from whom alone help can come.\n\n“But when He delivers them safely to shore, only some become relatively grateful.” The tragedy, the verse tells us, is what happens after the rescue. When God, in His mercy, brings them safely to land, not all of them remember. Only “some” become grateful, and even those only “relatively,” their gratitude incomplete and short-lived. The sincere devotion of the storm fades quickly on the shore, replaced by forgetfulness and a return to the old ways. This fickleness is the very pattern the verse exposes and laments.\n\n“And none rejects Our signs except whoever is deceitful, ungrateful.” The verse closes by naming the character of those who turn away after being saved. They are deceitful and ungrateful, treating the mercy they received as if it were nothing, and then having the audacity to reject the signs of the very God who rescued them. Their behaviour reveals a deep sickness of the heart: the inability to sustain gratitude, the readiness to forget mercy, the deceit that allows them to live as though they had never cried out for help.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a mirror held up to the human heart. It asks the uncomfortable question: do I remember God only in the storm and forget Him on the shore? The sincere devotion that rises in danger is good as far as it goes, but God deserves a devotion that does not depend on circumstances, a gratitude that survives the calm as surely as it surges in the crisis. The verse invites us to be among the truly grateful, those whose remembrance of God is steady, whose thankfulness does not fade when the waves subside, and whose worship is offered to Him alone in every season of life.",
  "33": "“O humanity! Be mindful of your Lord.” This verse opens with a universal address, “O humanity,” calling not just a single nation but all human beings to mindfulness of God. The call to “taqwa,” that living awareness of God that shapes thought and conduct, is directed at everyone, because everyone stands in the same relationship to the One Lord. Mindfulness is the foundation of all genuine religion, the posture from which every other duty flows.\n\n“And beware of a Day when no parent will be of any benefit to their child, nor will a child be of any benefit to their parent.” The verse then gives the reason for mindfulness: a Day is coming when the deepest human bonds will be of no avail. On that Day, a parent will not be able to help a child, nor a child a parent. The relationships that fill this life with meaning and security, the love of parent and child, will offer no protection. Each soul will stand alone, concerned only with its own account. This is not cruelty but justice: the Day strips away every false hope and leaves only the truth of what each person has done.\n\n“Surely Allah’s promise is true.” The verse anchors the warning in the certainty of God’s promise. The Day is not a maybe or a myth; it is a sure promise from the One who never breaks His word. To live as though that Day would never come is to live in denial of a guaranteed reality. The certainty of the promise is meant to wake us up, to make us order our present lives in light of an inescapable future.\n\n“So do not let the life of this world deceive you.” The verse closes with two warnings about deception. First, do not let the life of this world deceive you. The world, with its pleasures, distractions, and apparent permanence, is a master of illusion, persuading us that this is all there is, that there is no need to prepare, that the Day is far off or unreal. The verse calls us to see through that illusion.\n\n“Nor let the Chief Deceiver deceive you about Allah.” The second warning concerns Satan, called here the Chief Deceiver, who works to mislead people about God Himself, minimising His justice, mocking His warnings, or distorting His nature. The two deceptions work together: the world lulls us into heedlessness, and Satan whispers that God is other than He truly is. Against both, the verse sets the single remedy of mindfulness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an urgent call to wakefulness. It asks us to live with the reality of the Day always before us, to refuse the world’s sedation and the devil’s lies, and to cultivate a mindfulness of God so deep that neither the glitter of this life nor the whispers of deception can turn us from the truth, the truth of a Day when every soul will stand alone and only the awareness of God, lived out in faith and good deeds, will be of any benefit at all.",
  "34": "“Indeed, Allah alone has the knowledge of the Hour.” This verse gathers together a set of realities known only to God, and places them in striking contrast to human ignorance. The first is the knowledge of the Hour, the exact moment when this world will end and the Day of Judgment will begin. This knowledge is hidden from every creature. No prophet, no angel, no scholar, and no astrologer knows when it will come. It belongs to Allah alone. This should make every person humble about predicting the future and serious about preparing for it, since it could arrive at any time.\n\n“He sends down the rain, and knows what is in the wombs.” The verse then names two more areas of exclusive divine knowledge, drawn from everyday life. The sending of rain, on which all life depends, is in God’s hands; no human can command it or predict it with certainty. And what is in the wombs, the unseen forming of new life, its nature, its future, its destiny, is known fully only to God. These examples, taken from the most ordinary experiences of rain and birth, show that even the commonest events involve a knowledge that belongs to God alone.\n\n“No soul knows what it will earn for tomorrow.” The verse then turns to the future of each individual. No soul knows what it will gain or do on the very next day. Our plans, our hopes, our confident predictions about tomorrow are all wrapped in uncertainty. We do not know what opportunities or trials await us, what gains or losses, what joys or sorrows. This should cultivate both humility and dependence on God, the One who does know.\n\n“And no soul knows in what land it will die.” Finally, the verse names the greatest unknown of all: the place of one’s death. A person may live their whole life in one land and die in another; they may imagine a long future in a familiar place and be taken suddenly, far from home. The location and moment of death are hidden, known only to God.\n\n“Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware.” The verse closes by affirming the two attributes that encompass all these hidden things. God is All-Knowing, possessing the full content of every truth. And He is All-Aware, intimately familiar with every detail, inward and outward. What is hidden from us is completely open to Him.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to humble dependence on the One who knows what we cannot. Our ignorance of the Hour, the rain, the wombs, tomorrow, and our place of death should make us neither anxious nor reckless, but trusting and prepared. The verse invites us to release our grip on the illusion of control, to entrust the unknown future to the All-Knowing, All-Aware God, and to live each day in readiness, knowing that the only certainty we have is the present moment, and the only safeguard against the unknown is a heart turned in faith toward the One to whom all these hidden things are fully known."
};
